---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import { siteConfig } from "../../config";

const pageTitle = "Estimating stellar ages from the CMD with KD trees";
const pageDescription =
  "On assigning approximate ages to Gaia subgiant stars by matching them to PARSEC isochrones in a scaled five-dimensional space using a KDTree.";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
    <meta name="description" content={pageDescription} />
    <title>{pageTitle} | {siteConfig.name}</title>
  </head>
  <body class="blog-page">
    <main class="px-6 py-12 sm:px-10 md:px-14 md:py-16 lg:px-16 xl:px-20">
      <article class="blog-shell mx-auto max-w-5xl text-[1.02rem] leading-8">
        <a href="/#blog" class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-900">← Back to portfolio</a>

        <header class="mt-6 border-b border-gray-200 pb-10">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em]" style={`color: ${siteConfig.accentColor}`}>
            Blog
          </p>
          <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-bold leading-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-5xl">
            Estimating stellar ages from the CMD with KD trees
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-6 max-w-4xl text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            This blog post is about how I assign approximate ages to <em>Gaia</em> subgiant stars by matching them to PARSEC isochrones in a colour-magnitude diagram, while also using metallicity and atmospheric parameters as secondary constraints. The goal is not precision age dating for individual stars, but a robust way to carve out clean, disjoint age populations that can be compared against simulations.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-sm text-gray-600">
            <span class="px-3 py-1.5">Gaia DR3</span>
            <span class="px-3 py-1.5">PARSEC Isochrones</span>
            <span class="px-3 py-1.5">KDTree</span>
            <span class="px-3 py-1.5">CMD Fitting</span>
            <span class="px-3 py-1.5">Galactic Dynamics</span>
          </div>
        </header>

        <section class="mt-14">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Why use the subgiant branch?</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            <p>
              Isochrone fitting can be a fairly blunt instrument if you try to age-date stars across the whole Hertzsprung-Russell diagram. On the main sequence and giant branch, different combinations of age, mass, and metallicity can sit frustratingly close together. The subgiant branch is better in the sense that the isochrones are more strongly ordered by age, so approximate age assignment becomes much more defensible.
            </p>
            <p>
              That still does not make the ages exact. A full Bayesian treatment would be more principled if the aim were to infer precise stellar ages star by star. But for my use case, I only need age bins that are narrow enough to compare against simulation snapshots and broad enough to remain statistically useful. In that regime, a fast nearest-neighbour interpolation scheme works surprisingly well.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">The basic idea</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            <p>
            Classical isochrone fitting usually means matching each star to the single evolutionary track or isochrone that fits best in a colour-magnitude diagram. That can work reasonably well, but it can also become brittle when different combinations of age and metallicity land in similar parts of the diagram. In my approach, I compare each star to a local cloud of nearby PARSEC model points and take a weighted average over those neighbours. That makes the age assignment smoother, more robust to noise, and less sensitive to the exact sampling of the model grid. I also add a few extra observables beyond CMD position — metallicity, surface gravity, and effective temperature — to help reduce degeneracy between otherwise similar-looking stars. These do not replace the CMD as the main anchor of the fit, but they provide useful secondary constraints when different isochrones overlap. So the method is still simple and fast, but a little more physically informed than plain CMD-only matching.
            </p>
            <p>
              The model grid is made from PARSEC isochrone points restricted to the same subgiant selection box. I compare each observed star to that grid in a scaled metric so that position in the CMD drives the match, while metallicity and atmospheric parameters act as secondary constraints.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr,0.95fr]">
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Why a KD-tree helps</h2>
            <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
              Once the isochrone grid is expressed in scaled coordinates, the task becomes a nearest-neighbour search in five dimensions. Doing that naively for hundreds of thousands of stars is wasteful. A KD-tree gives a fast way to query the local patch of model space that matters for each star.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
              In practice I query the 128 nearest model points, keep only neighbours within a scaled distance threshold, and convert those local matches into a smoothed age estimate rather than assigning every star to a single best-fitting isochrone point.
            </p>
          </div>
          <aside class="p-6">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-900">Feature scales</h3>
            <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-gray-700">
              <li><strong>Colour:</strong> <code>0.08</code></li>
              <li><strong>Absolute magnitude:</strong> <code>0.12</code></li>
              <li><strong>Metallicity:</strong> <code>0.4</code></li>
              <li><strong>Surface gravity:</strong> <code>0.4</code></li>
              <li><strong>log temperature:</strong> <code>0.2</code></li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-600">
              These values are chosen so the matching is dominated by CMD location, with the extra parameters helping to break degeneracies.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">The role of metallicity</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            <p>
              The extra dimensions matter because mono-abundance isochrones in the subgiant region are well separated, but isochrones of different metallicity can overlap in the CMD as shown in the Figure below. If I only matched in colour and magnitude, stars with different chemistry could be pulled toward the wrong age sequence.
            </p>
            <p>
              I therefore use calibrated <em>Gaia</em> DR3 GSP-Phot metallicities where available, with the remaining stars falling back to the raw catalogue values. Observed metallicities are clipped to the model-grid range so the method never extrapolates beyond the available isochrones.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-1">
            <figure class="overflow-visible bg-transparent">
              <img src="/images/blog/kdtree-stars/sgb_cmd_with_isochrones.png" alt="Subgiant-branch colour-magnitude diagram with PARSEC isochrones overlaid at different metallicities and ages" class="w-[85%] mx-auto" />
              <figcaption class="px-6 py-5 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-600">
                <strong>CMD structure in the subgiant branch.</strong> The subgiant branch is relatively age-sensitive, but metallicity still shifts the isochrone family enough that it should not be ignored.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">From neighbours to age probabilities</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            <p>
              I do not use a hard nearest-neighbour assignment. Instead, the nearby model points are given Gaussian weights, which keeps the interpolation local but smooth.
            </p>
            <p>
              Because the PARSEC grid is not sampled uniformly in age and metallicity, I also correct for the local density of model points so that densely sampled isochrones do not dominate by accident.
            </p>
            <p>
              The final normalised weights are accumulated into broad age bins, and I also compute an expected age from the weighted mean in log age.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">A compact implementation sketch</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            The full pipeline has a fair amount of catalogue plumbing, but the core of the method is compact. I’ve kept the code blocks here in a more demo-like style, focusing on the three ideas that matter most: building the model grid, querying neighbours, and turning neighbours into age probabilities.
          </p>

          <div class="demo-code-card mt-8">
            <div class="demo-code-body overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code><span class="tok-keyword">from</span> <span class="tok-module">sklearn.neighbors</span> <span class="tok-keyword">import</span> <span class="tok-class">KDTree</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">import</span> <span class="tok-module">numpy</span> <span class="tok-keyword">as</span> np

X_model <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.vstack([
    color,
    MG,
    mh,
    logg,
    logTeff,
]).T
y_model <span class="tok-operator">=</span> logAge

<span class="tok-comment"># y_model is logAge for each model point</span>
<span class="tok-comment"># X_model[:,2] is MH for each model point (stacked as [color, MG, MH, logg, Teff])</span>
logA_key <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.round(y_model, <span class="tok-number">3</span>) <span class="tok-comment"># logAge grid rounding</span>
mh_key <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.round(X_model[:, <span class="tok-number">2</span>], <span class="tok-number">3</span>) <span class="tok-comment"># MH grid rounding</span>

keys <span class="tok-operator">=</span> logA_key.astype(str) <span class="tok-operator">+</span> <span class="tok-string">"_"</span> <span class="tok-operator">+</span> mh_key.astype(str)
uniq, counts <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.unique(keys, return_counts<span class="tok-operator">=</span><span class="tok-constant">True</span>)
count_map <span class="tok-operator">=</span> dict(zip(uniq, counts))

<span class="tok-comment"># feature scales - normalise each feature by its typical scale</span>
sig <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.array([<span class="tok-number">0.08</span>, <span class="tok-number">0.12</span>, <span class="tok-number">0.4</span>, <span class="tok-number">0.4</span>, <span class="tok-number">0.2</span>]) <span class="tok-comment"># color, MG, [M/H], logg, logTeff</span>

tree <span class="tok-operator">=</span> <span class="tok-class">KDTree</span>(X_model <span class="tok-operator">/</span> sig, leaf_size<span class="tok-operator">=</span><span class="tok-number">200</span>)</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-8 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            Then the observed stars are queried in the same scaled space and converted into local Gaussian weights.
          </p>

          <div class="demo-code-card mt-8">
            <div class="demo-code-body overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code><span class="tok-comment"># Observed values</span>
X_star <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.vstack([C0, MG0, mh_u, lg0, Tf0]).T
X_star[good, <span class="tok-number">2</span>] <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.clip(X_star[good, <span class="tok-number">2</span>], mh_min, mh_max)

dist, ind <span class="tok-operator">=</span> tree.query(X_star[good] <span class="tok-operator">/</span> sig, k<span class="tok-operator">=</span><span class="tok-number">128</span>)
mask <span class="tok-operator">=</span> dist <span class="tok-operator">&lt;</span> <span class="tok-number">2.0</span>
w <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.exp(<span class="tok-operator">-</span><span class="tok-number">0.5</span> <span class="tok-operator">*</span> dist<span class="tok-operator">**</span><span class="tok-number">2</span>) <span class="tok-operator">*</span> mask</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-8 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            Finally, the weights are corrected for non-uniform model density and accumulated into age bins.
          </p>

          <div class="demo-code-card mt-8">
            <div class="demo-code-body overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code><span class="tok-comment"># Inverse-count weights =&gt; each (logAge, MH) isochrone has equal total weight</span>
w_model <span class="tok-operator">=</span> np.array([<span class="tok-number">1.0</span> <span class="tok-operator">/</span> count_map[k] <span class="tok-keyword">for</span> k <span class="tok-keyword">in</span> keys], dtype<span class="tok-operator">=</span><span class="tok-class">float</span>)

alpha <span class="tok-operator">=</span> <span class="tok-number">0.5</span>
w <span class="tok-operator">*=</span> (w_model[ind] <span class="tok-operator">**</span> alpha)
w <span class="tok-operator">/=</span> w.sum(axis<span class="tok-operator">=</span><span class="tok-number">1</span>)[:, <span class="tok-constant">None</span>]

<span class="tok-comment"># Loop over age bins and sum the neighbour weights falling into each one</span>
<span class="tok-keyword">for</span> i <span class="tok-keyword">in</span> range(len(bins) <span class="tok-operator">-</span> <span class="tok-number">1</span>):
    sel <span class="tok-operator">=</span> (logAge_nn <span class="tok-operator">&gt;=</span> bins[i]) <span class="tok-operator">&amp;</span> (logAge_nn <span class="tok-operator">&lt;</span> bins[i<span class="tok-operator">+</span><span class="tok-number">1</span>])
    P[:, i] <span class="tok-operator">=</span> (w <span class="tok-operator">*</span> sel).sum(axis<span class="tok-operator">=</span><span class="tok-number">1</span>) <span class="tok-operator">/</span> w.sum(axis<span class="tok-operator">=</span><span class="tok-number">1</span>)</code></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Results</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            <p>
              This figure shows the same subgiant selection box in CMD space, but now each star is coloured by the expected age returned by the KD-tree interpolation. The smooth colour gradient is the main sanity check: younger stars lie systematically toward one part of the subgiant branch, while older stars occupy another, with the isochrone sequence running through the sample in the expected direction. In other words, the method is recovering a coherent age ordering rather than assigning ages in a noisy or patchy way.
            </p>
            <figure class="overflow-visible bg-transparent">
              <img src="/images/blog/kdtree-stars/sgb_age_map_expected_age.png" alt="Subgiant-branch stars in the CMD coloured by expected age from KD-tree isochrone matching" class="w-[85%] mx-auto" />
              <figcaption class="px-6 py-5 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-600">
                <strong>Expected age map.</strong> After local interpolation in the scaled feature space, the CMD shows a smooth age ordering across the subgiant selection region.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-20 border-t border-gray-200 pt-10">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">References</h2>
          <ol class="mt-8 list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            <li>Bressan, A., Marigo, P., Girardi, L., et al. (2012), <em>PARSEC: stellar tracks and isochrones</em>.</li>
            <li>Pont, F. &amp; Eyer, L. (2004), <em>Estimating stellar ages from evolutionary tracks and observational data</em>.</li>
            <li>Jørgensen, B. R. &amp; Lindegren, L. (2005), <em>Astrophysical parameters from Bayesian estimation</em>.</li>
            <li>Andrae, R., Fouesneau, M., Creevey, O., et al. (2023), <em>Gaia DR3 astrophysical parameters from GSP-Phot</em>.</li>
            <li>Friedman, J. H. (1991), <em>Multivariate adaptive regression splines</em>.</li>
            <li>Pedregosa, F., Varoquaux, G., Gramfort, A., et al. (2011), <em>scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python</em>.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>
      </article>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
